===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|physiology|historical}} A [[sperm cell]] or [[spermatozoon]]; also, the [[embryo]] that was formerly thought to be [[contain]]ed inside a spermatozoon in a [[fully]] [[developed#Adjective|developed]] [[state#Noun|state]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David M. Friedman|chapter=The Gear Shift|title=A Mind of Its Own: A Cultural History of the Penis|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Free Press (publisher)|The Free Press]]|year=2001|page=76|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=dYYx-sHniI8C&pg=PA76|isbn=978-0-684-85320-8|passage=[[w:Antonie van Leeuwenhoek|[Antonie van] Leeuwenhoek]]'s most mysterious finding was yet to come, however. Inside the '''animalcules''' in the thickest part of the semen he saw / all manner of great and small vessels, so various and so numerous that I do not doubt that they be nerves, arteries and veins.&nbsp;…}}
# {{lb|en|zoology|archaic}} A [[microscopic]] [[aquatic#Adjective|aquatic]] [[animal#Noun|animal]], including [[protozoan|protozoa]] and [[rotifer]]s. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Jeremiah Sullivan|authorlink=John Jeremiah Sullivan|chapter=La·hwi·ne·ski: Career of an Eccentric Naturalist|title={{w|Pulphead}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|Farrar, Straus and Giroux}}|year=2011|pages=212–213|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/pulpheadessays0000sull/page/213/mode/1up|oclc=978-0-374-53290-1|passage=If we are part of nature, then we are synonymous with it at the metaphysical level, every bit as much as the first all-but-inorganic '''animalcules''' that ever formed a chain of themselves in the blow hole of a primordial sea vent.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[small#Adjective|small]] animal. {{defdate|16th|20th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Carlyle Sartor Resartus|chapter=Pause|page=137|passage=Why, there is not a Man, or a Thing, now alive but has tools. The basest of created '''animalcules''', the Spider itself, has a spinning-jenny, and warping-mill, and power-loom within its head: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Challenger|page=555|passage=Is it not evident that if a parasitic '''animalcule''' desired to call its attention it would sink a hole in its shell and so stimulate its sensory apparatus?}}

